UID:6a91e758b204780029964c8b@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:*This program has been CANCELLED due to unforeseen circumstanc
 es.*\nHave you ever wondered what's going on inside a computer? Ever wante
 d to see a computer taken apart? This workshop is your chance! During this
  workshop\, we'll go over what's inside a computer\, how to properly open 
 a desktop\, and safety guidelines to follow when it comes to working with 
 electronics. Q&A included.\nAll teens are welcome!\nThis presentation is h
 osted by Free Geek Toronto\, a non-profit organization that refurbishes d
 iscarded computers and makes them useful tools again for people who need t
 hem.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this for
 m is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Ac
 t\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used 
 for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions ab
 out how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline
  staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto
  ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Morningside\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a91e758b204780029964c8b
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Teens
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Young Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a8212a8aafa6100295d0432"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_young_adults
SUMMARY:Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T200000
UID:6a8212a8aafa6100295d0432@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Come join the readers at Long Branch each month. We share opin
 ions and thoughts of the books selected. For the Month of October take a l
 ook at "Children of the Moon" by Anthony DeSa.\nFrom critically-acclaimed 
 author Anthony De Sa\, a raw and compelling story of love\, war and the di
 fferences that both divide and unite us. In the wispy grasslands of Tanzan
 ia in the 1950s\, a Maasai woman gives birth to a miracle. The other villa
 gers see the baby as a curse. The baby--a daughter\, named Kibo--has skin 
 as white as bone\, and from her first moments on earth\, she is forced to 
 claim her life in spite of the violence she incites. As she grows\, Kibo s
 heds her identity to become Liloe\, and then Po\, continuously trying to a
 djust herself so that she can fit into the world around her. Further south
 \, in Portuguese-controlled Mozambique\, a young boy\, Ezequiel\, has been
  adopted by two missionaries. Zeca\, too\, struggles with a lack of accept
 ance. Not recognized by his Portuguese father's community\, or by his Mako
 nde mother's tribe\, Zeca lives his life on the borders of both worlds. An
 d when civil war erupts in Mozambique\, Zeca must choose what--and who--he
  is willing to leave behind\nNo Registration Required. Drop in.\nAll Welco
 me\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form 
 is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\
 , sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used fo
 r event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions abou
 t how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline s
 taff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto O
 N M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Long Branch\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a8212a8aafa6100295d0432
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"YOHOMO": "https://www.yohomo.ca/event/connection-beyond-breath-living-relationships-with-our-dead"}
X-SOURCE-ID:yohomo
SUMMARY:Connection Beyond Breath: Living Relationships with Our Dead
DTSTART:20261027T190000
DTEND:20261027T190000
UID:df67f60328935e3eb9a2454fd162122b@yohomo.ca
DESCRIPTION:Queer Death Salon is a virtual community space for all 2SLGBTQ
 IA+ people to come together to discuss death\, dying and grief. In this co
 llective conversation\, grievers are invited to connect over ways they are
  or want to be in relation with their dead. This is not an instructional s
 ession but rather a container for folks to share and hear about the variet
 y of ways our dead show up in our lives - in dreams\, memories\, conversat
 ions\, spaces\, through animal sightings\, cultural and spiritual practice
 s\, etc. - and how ongoing relationships with our dead can transform how w
 e live\, in tough\, tender\, and touching ways. Fueled by years of cultiva
 ting relationships with their dead\, Case hopes participants leave this co
 nversation both feeling more resourced to build and maintain relationships
  with theirs\, and imagining how we\, too\, will want to be remembered and
  connected with when we join them. “Time is queer and so is grief: grief
  queers time inexorably. Lose a friend and find how the lines between life
  and death are also a perpetual spectrum. Who knew loving our dead and nam
 ing our dying while trying to survive could be so fucking queer? I would l
 ove to meet you here.” ‍
LOCATION:Online\, online
URL:https://www.yohomo.ca/event/connection-beyond-breath-living-relationsh
 ips-with-our-dead
X-SOURCE:YOHOMO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Luma Toronto Discover": "https://luma.com/duvhkqe3"}
X-SOURCE-ID:luma_toronto_discover
SUMMARY:Luxury... and Make It Loud. Self-Fashioning in Black\, Latinx & Qu
 eer Communities. by nigel lezama
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T191000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T210000
UID:evt-Z54VdeDkDLHdjU8@events.lu.ma
DESCRIPTION:Address:\n46 Ossington Ave\nToronto\, ON\nCanada\n\n✨ A Spec
 ial Edition of The Fashion & Theory Book Club ✨\nThis October\, we're jo
 ined by nigel lezama\, Associate Professor of Fashion at TMU\, for a conve
 rsation on his new book chapter\, "Luxury... and Make It Loud: Self-Fashio
 ning in Black\, Latinx & Queer Communities."\nlezama's work explores luxur
 y from a fresh perspective\, examining how Black\, Latinx and queer commun
 ities have used style and self-expression to create their own versions of 
 luxury and challenge the mainstream.\nThe most exciting opportunity to rea
 d the work and then talk about it with the author. Can't wait for this…\
 n\nHosted by Fashion Book Club
LOCATION:46 Ossington Ave\, Toronto\, ON M6J 2Y7\, Canada
URL:https://luma.com/duvhkqe3
