BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Engineering Engineering Events": "https://www.engineering.utoronto.ca/event/crash-course-for-building-leading-teams/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:engineering_engineering_events
X-SOURCE:UofT Engineering
DTSTART;TZID=UTC:20261029T100000
DTEND;TZID=UTC:20261029T150000
DTSTAMP:20261014T192521Z
CREATED:20261014T192411Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261014T192521Z
UID:10004778-1793268000-1793286000@www.engineering.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:Crash Course for Building & Leading Teams
DESCRIPTION:Thursday\, October 29 | 10:00 AM – 3:00 PM | Register in Advance \nDesigned for current FASE club leaders\, this interactive workshop will strengthen your team building and management skills through a leadership-focused lens. Participants will explore strategies for creating inclusive team environments\, fostering engagement\, and leading teams effectively toward shared goals. While the workshop is structured as a two-part series\, participants are welcome to attend either session individually or join both for the full experience.  \nDate: Thursday\, October 29  \nTime: 10:00 AM – 3:00 PM  \nLocation: St. George campus (exact location to be confirmed upon acceptance).   \n*Lunch and snacks will be provided.   \nPart I (10:00am-12:00pm): Building Inclusive Teams  \nPart II (1:00pm-3:00pm): Leading Effective Teams  \n\nWorkshop spaces are limited and all applicants will be contacted to confirm their acceptance.  \n\n\nIt is expected that accepted participants prioritize program attendance\, except in case of extenuating circumstances (e.g. illness) in which case staff should be informed in advance.  \n\nProgram Registration closes on Sunday\, October 25th\, 2026 at 11:59pm  \nQuestions? Vivian Trumblay\, Leadership Education Specialist vivian.trumblay@utoronto.ca   \nISTEP Crash Course for Building & Leading Teams – Fill out form 
URL:https://www.engineering.utoronto.ca/event/crash-course-for-building-leading-teams/
LOCATION:St. George campus (exact location to be confirmed upon acceptance)
CATEGORIES:Lectures, seminars and workshops
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre": "https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/10/exophonia"}
X-SOURCE-ID:jccc
SUMMARY:The Exophonia
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20270930T190000
UID:5274f7577ce4a54272784e345c03604a@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:Xuan Ye’s animation\, The Exphonia \, transforms the ICNALE 
 corpus\, hundreds of L2 (second language) English essays on part‑time jo
 bs and smoking bans\, into a living monument to exophony\, the act of spea
 king and writing in a non‑native tongue. Each essay becomes a root syste
 m that germinates\, forks\, and twists in real time. Grammatical deviation
 s and non‑native word combinations physically warp the tendrils and dive
 rge the rhizomes. The metaphor of the rhizome is reimagined as a cross‑c
 ultural ecology in which linguistic markedness is not a flaw\, but the ver
 y soil from which an astonishing forest emerges. Developed through a resid
 ency at the JCCC over the summer of 2026\, Exophonia acts as a companion p
 iece to the Many Voices: Japanese Languages in Canada exhibit in the Moriy
 ama Nikkei Heritage Centre\, extending its insights on linguistic non-stan
 dardness and non-nativeness to our hyper-globalized reality. Artist bio: X
 uan Ye makes noise as a compass\, navigating the intersections of art\, mu
 sic\, and technology. Their practice unfolds across software\, sound\, ima
 ge\, installation\, performance\, publication\, and teaching\, tracing the
  diffraction and resonance across technological\, biological\, and ecologi
 cal systems. Their work has been exhibited at the Centre for Culture and T
 echnology (2025)\, Kamias Triennial (2024)\, MOCA Toronto (2022)\, UCCA Sh
 anghai (2022)\, Venice Architecture Biennale (2021)\, MUTEK Montreal (2021
 )\, InterAccess (2020)\, and Goethe-Institut Beijing (2018)\, among others
 . They have been recognized with residencies at the Banff Centre for Arts 
 and Creativity (2026)\, University of Toronto (2025/26)\, ZHdK (2024)\, an
 d Pro Helvetia (2023)\, and have received grants from the Canada Council f
 or the Arts\, Ontario Arts Council\, Toronto Arts Council\, Partners in Ar
 t\, and SSHRC. Their work has been featured in Peripheral Review\, Canadia
 n Art\, ArtAsiaPacific\, KUNSTFORUM International\, and Musicworks. Origin
 ally from China\, Ye lives in Toronto/Tkaronto and is an Assistant Profess
 or at the University of Waterloo.\n\nCategories: Exhibition
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/10/exophonia
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre": "https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/10/many-voices-japanese-languages-canada"}
X-SOURCE-ID:jccc
SUMMARY:Many Voices: Japanese Languages in Canada
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20270930T190000
UID:af75defeeaf9e20ef834c760dbe3f179@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:Japanese languages have been spoken in Canada since the late 1
 800s. From nisei nihongo to language schools to the rise of Japanese as a 
 global language\, Many Voices: Japanese Languages in Canada explores how J
 apanese languages have been used through multiple waves of migration\, set
 tlement\, and displacement. Featuring ethnographic comics by Dr. Ai Tanigu
 chi based on interviews with Japanese Canadians\, the exhibit celebrates t
 he breadth of experience with the language amongst the Japanese Canadian c
 ommunity. The exhibit's companion new media art piece\, The Exophonia by X
 uan Ye\, interrogates the permeability of native and non-native speaker di
 stinctions through the metaphor of a rhizome stemming from linguistic mark
 edness. Check it out here . Many Voices: Japanese Languages in Canada was 
 developed in partnership the Canadian Language Museum and the University o
 f Toronto Mississauga. Please join us for the opening reception on October
  29! Find more information here . Images: 2. A Group Portrait of Toronto J
 apanese Language School\, 1960. JCCC George Tsushima Collection\, 2011.298
 .01.11. 3. Kohaku Uta Gassen at JCCC\, ca. 1980s. JCCC Tomiko Nishimura Co
 llection 2016-24-11-103. 4. Toronto Japanese Language School registration 
 form\, ca. 1960s. JCCC Coby Kobayashi Collection\, 2007.04.03.143.\n\nCate
 gories: Exhibition
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/10/many-voices-japanese-languages-canada
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Bicycling Network": "https://www.tbn.ca/widget/event-6876872"}
X-SOURCE-ID:toronto_bicycling_network
SUMMARY:City Walk - Chinatown East and Little India
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T130000
UID:4d714a7ec8e17b51cb1fabc49cc29d3f@tbn.ca
DESCRIPTION:Meet at about 10:00 AM at the main entrance of Broadview Stati
 on\, at street level for the first Thursday walk of the Fall 2026 season. 
 We will begin the walk at 10:10 sharp. We will walk in a loop for about 8 
 Km \, see some interesting Toronto neighbourhoods\, and return to the star
 t. We will walk south to Chinatown East\, then east along Gerrard St to Li
 ttle India\, and north to The Danforth\, where we can stop for a coffee an
 d a pastry. We will then walk west back to Broadview. The walk will be ent
 irely on city streets\, so please wear shoes or boots appropriate for the 
 weather. There are no scheduled subway outages for this date but please ch
 eck https://www.ttc.ca/service-advisories/subway-serv...
LOCATION:the main entrance of Broadview Station\, at street level for the 
 first Thursday walk of the Fall 2026 season
URL:https://www.tbn.ca/widget/event-6876872
X-SOURCE:Toronto Bicycling Network
END:VEVENT
