BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-ID:gcal_4h7dspldpuviiv4o1slj9o5p5c
X-SOURCE:CITA Seminars
DTSTART:20261029T180000Z
DTEND:20261029T190000Z
DTSTAMP:20261024T043526Z
UID:0hdidgs86d3juaauhum6qcvplk@google.com
CREATED:20260825T181711Z
DESCRIPTION:<p>Title: <b>Why a Tiny Neutrino Inspired Me to Reconstruct the
  Entire Universe with AI</b></p>\n\n<p>Abstract: Measuring neutrino mass is
  a central goal of particle physics and cosmology. Its imprint on cosmic st
 ructure offers a route to that measurement\, but extracting this informatio
 n requires inference that can handle high-dimensional data\, expensive simu
 lations\, and multimodal datasets. AI is transforming how we address these 
 challenges.\n\nIn this talk\, I will motivate field-level inference\, which
  uses the full spatial information in cosmic maps to reconstruct the Univer
 se’s initial conditions and optimally infer cosmological parameters. I will
  show how neural networks improve reconstruction and measurements of baryon
  acoustic oscillations using data from the DESI galaxy survey\, and discuss
  the challenge of ensuring that AI-based inference remains robust\, interpr
 etable\, and efficient. I will also showcase recent work using large langua
 ge models to discover and improve reconstruction algorithms and accelerate 
 science more broadly.\n\nBeyond galaxy surveys\, the next generation of cos
 mological discovery will be defined by combining information from multiple 
 cosmological datasets—including weak lensing and the cosmic microwave backg
 round—to tighten constraints\, mitigate systematics\, and detect otherwise 
 undetectable signals. I will end by highlighting work from the Simons Obser
 vatory’s Sky Modeling group to enable such joint analyses and accelerate fu
 ndamental physics discovery over the coming decade.</p>\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20261014T190233Z
LOCATION:1318A
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Adrian Bayer (Flatiron Institute\, CCA)
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
