BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events": "https://events.yorku.ca/events/the-spectre-of-the-crowd-liberal-democracy-and-the-fear-of-the-masses/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events
SUMMARY:The spectre of the crowd: Liberal democracy and the fear of the ma
 sses
DTSTART:20261104T213000Z
DTEND:20261104T233000Z
DTSTAMP:20261019T214900Z
UID:MEC-1455fe8974773676829466004e600e64@events.yorku.ca
RECURRENCE-ID:20261104T213000Z
SEQUENCE:2
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://events.yorku.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/
 10/Poster-101.jpg
CATEGORIES:Faculty of Environmental &amp\; Urban Change,Faculty of Graduat
 e Studies,Graduate Program in Environmental Studies,Graduate Program in Po
 litical Science,Research &amp\; Innovation,YFile newsletter
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20261019
DESCRIPTION:EUC Seminar: The spectre of the crowd: Liberal democracy and t
 he fear of the masses with Dr. Hannah Richter\nThis talk will present key 
 arguments and first findings from ongoing research into the way crowd psyc
 hology’s idea of ‘the masses’ has shaped the political imaginary of 
 liberal democracy. At the end of the 19th century\, crowd psychologists li
 ke Hippolyte Taine\, Gabriel Tarde and Gustave Le Bon sought to deduce law
 s of collective behaviour from the political upheavals of their time. The 
 crowd they presented as the result of their pseudo-scientific investigatio
 ns was irrational\, primitive and readily susceptible to the whisperings o
 f political strongmen. In the following decades\, and despite their obviou
 s biases\, the political ideas of crowd psychology came to resonate with a
  diverse group of scholars grappling with fast-paced technological\, polit
 ical and social changes. Amongst them were Joseph Schumpeter\, Hannah Aren
 dt and Sigmund Freud. Regardless of whether mass participation is rejected
  or endorsed\, I argue that all three endow the masses with a constitutive
 \, dangerous irrationality that the political community must keep at bay. 
 I will further show how\, filtered through the work of José Ortega y Gass
 et\, crowd psychological ideas of mass behaviour shaped the demophobic dis
 course of the early Mont Pelerin Society meetings\, and influenced the eco
 nomic writings of Friedrich Hayek and Ludwig von Mises. To conclude\, I wi
 ll reflect on the relevance of interrogating the intellectual legacy of cr
 owd psychology’s political ideas in the context of what Cas Mudde has te
 rmed the ‘populist zeitgeist\,’ in which evaluations of collective pol
 itical behaviour are ever-present.\nSpeaker Bio\nHannah Richter is Assista
 nt Professor in Politics at the University of Sussex\, UK\, where she rese
 arches and teaches critical political thought\, and Editor-in-Chief of Dis
 tinktion: Journal of Social Theory. She is the author of The Politics of O
 rientation. Deleuze meets Luhmann (SUNY Press\, 2023) and Challenging Anth
 ropocene Ontology. Modernity\, Ecology and Indigenous Complexities (Blooms
 bury\, 2024\; with E. Randazzo). Her work has been published in academic o
 utlets including the European Journal of Political Theory\, the European J
 ournal of Social Theory\, Globalizations\, International Political Sociolo
 gy and Political Geography. At the moment\, she is working on a project th
 at develops a critical genealogy of how 20th century democratic thinking i
 magines ‘the masses’.\nEVERYONE WELCOME!\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20261019
LOCATION:HNES 142 @ 4700 Keele St\, Toronto\, ON M3J 1P3\, Canada
PRIORITY:5
TRANSP:OPAQUE
URL:https://events.yorku.ca/events/the-spectre-of-the-crowd-liberal-democr
 acy-and-the-fear-of-the-masses/
X-SOURCE:York University
END:VEVENT
