BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events": "https://events.yorku.ca/events/crs-tubman-book-launch-unwelcome-shores-black-refugees-in-america/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events
SUMMARY:CRS/Tubman Book Launch: Unwelcome Shores: Black Refugees in Americ
 a
DTSTART:20261105T180000Z
DTEND:20261105T193000Z
DTSTAMP:20261014T154800Z
UID:MEC-3c12c84af346626dc2f1b77e52bb301e@events.yorku.ca
RECURRENCE-ID:20261105T180000Z
SEQUENCE:3
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://events.yorku.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/
 10/book-cover.jpg
CATEGORIES:ORUs
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20261014
DESCRIPTION:November 5\, 2026\n1:00 – 2:30pm (ET/Toronto)\nThis is a vir
 tual event\nZoom link: https://yorku.zoom.us/meeting/register/B8h3NDjKS16Q
 hZnv-BRSHA ( https://yorku.zoom.us/meeting/register/B8h3NDjKS16QhZnv-BRSHA
  )\nGuest speaker: Bernadette Ludwig\, Assistant Dean\, Macricostas School
  of Arts and Sciences\, Western Connecticut State University\n\n\nUnwelcom
 e Shores. Black Refugees in America (Rutgers University Press) ( https://w
 ww.rutgersuniversitypress.org/unwelcome-shores/9781978843073 )\, Unwelcome
  Shores is an ethnographic study of the Liberian refugee community in Sta
 ten Island\, New York\, home to the largest per capita concentration of Li
 berians in the U.S.\, that sheds light on the racialization of Black refu
 gees and the racism they have experienced at every step of their migration
  journey. In this pioneering study\, sociologist Bernadette Ludwig explore
 s how Liberians have responded to such racist exclusions\, noting how memb
 ers of this community reject the informal refugee label once they are rese
 ttled in the United States. Liberian migrants often view the label as a li
 ability since the larger general public\, the media\, and the U.S. governm
 ent tend to regard Black refugees as an economic and social burden unworth
 y of assistance. Indeed\, Black refugees’ humanity is often ignored\, Lu
 dwig contends\, in favor of overemphasizing presumed barbaric violence\, e
 ndemic wars\, cultural backwardness\, and diseases. By detailing the lack 
 of aid and support for Black refugees and describing how Liberian refugees
  in particular have had to overcome various struggles and barriers in comi
 ng to the U.S. and while living here\, Unwelcome Shores highlights the ov
 erarching role of race and anti-Black racism in American society.\n\n\nBio
 graphy\n\n\n\nBernadette Ludwig\, PhD is a Sociologist and the Assistant 
 Dean of the Macricostas School of Arts and Sciences at Western Connecticut
  State University (WCSU). Prior to joining WCSU\, she was a tenured Profes
 sor of Sociology and the Director of Civic Engagement at a college in New 
 York.\nHer primary research focuses on the intersection of immigration\, r
 ace\, and gender and analyzes how seemingly racially neutral refugee and
  immigration policies indeed are inherently racist. In addition to her boo
 k\, Unwelcome Shores: Black Refugees in America  ( https://www.rutgersun
 iversitypress.org/unwelcome-shores/9781978843073/ )(Rutgers University Pre
 ss\, 2025) she had published widely in journals such as International M
 igration\, African and Black Diaspora: An International Journal\, and I
 nternational Journal of Migration\, Health and Social Care\, and in edite
 d books.\nIn another research project\, she investigates the significance 
 and meaning of community-engaged learning opportunities for students\, in 
 particular those from historically excluded groups which have been publish
 ed in Michigan Journal of Community Service\, Learning Science Education 
 & Civic Engagement\, and others. Her co-edited book How to Facilitate Com
 munity-Engaged Learning (Edward Elgar Publishing) was published in August
  2026.\nBefore pursuing a PhD in Sociology\, Dr. Ludwig worked with refuge
 e and immigrant communities in Atlanta GA. During that time\, she was the 
 executive director of two non-profit organizations\, including Culture Con
 nect\, Inc.\, which she co-founded. Her years of working in the field with
  refugees and immigrants continue to inform her research and her teaching.
   As a first-generation college graduate and immigrant\, she is committed
  to mentoring students\, in particular those from historically excluded gr
 oups. Dr. Ludwig also serves as a pro-bono country conditions expert for a
 sylum and deportation claims/hearings.\n\n\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20261014
ORGANIZER;CN="Centre for Refugee Studies":MAILTO:
PRIORITY:5
TRANSP:OPAQUE
URL:https://events.yorku.ca/events/crs-tubman-book-launch-unwelcome-shores
 -black-refugees-in-america/
X-SOURCE:York University
END:VEVENT
