ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://events.yorku.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/1
 0/Uplifted-by-the-Sea-The-Efik-of-Calabar-in-the-Atlantic-Slave-Trade.png
CATEGORIES:ORUs
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20261023
DESCRIPTION:Date: Thursday\, November 5\, 2026\nTime: 2:00-3:30pm EST\nLoc
 ation: Tubman Resource Room (314 York Lanes)\n4700 Keele Street \nToronto
 \, ON | M3J 1P3 \nIn-Person Registration: https://research.apps01.yorku.
 ca/machform/view.php?id=322950\nZoom Registration: https://yorku.zoom.us/m
 eeting/register/fDGGCUPYQHu66fmEakVbUg\n\n\n\n\n\nTitle: Uplifted by the S
 ea: The Efik of Calabar in the Atlantic Slave Trade\n \nAbstract:\nThe hi
 story of the Efik of Calabar illustrates how geography\, commerce\, and hu
 man exploitation shaped an African society’s fortunes and its place in t
 he Atlantic world. From the seventeenth century\, the Efik leveraged their
  strategic maritime location to establish Calabar as a major slave-trading
  port in the Bight of Biafra\, linking British merchants with hinterland c
 ommunities whose people were sold into Atlantic slavery. This paper examin
 es how maritime access\, commercial monopolies\, credit networks\, and ind
 igenous institutions such as the Ekpe society enabled Efik commercial domi
 nance. It explores the paradox of Efik resistance to the enslavement of th
 eir own people alongside their participation in the enslavement of neighbo
 uring communities. It also considers how Atlantic commerce transformed Efi
 k society through European goods\, literacy\, education\, and new expressi
 ons of wealth and prestige. Finally\, it interrogates the selective rememb
 rance of slavery in contemporary Calabar\, particularly the tendency to em
 phasize European culpability while obscuring indigenous participation. Dra
 wing on primary and secondary sources\, the paper argues that Efik prosper
 ity and regional prominence were inseparable from the Atlantic slave econo
 my. Ultimately\, the sea uplifted the Efik into extraordinary commercial a
 nd political prominence\, but their ascent was entangled with the exploita
 tion and suffering of others. Confronting this history offers a more nuanc
 ed understanding of African agency\, complicity\, and responsibility in th
 e Atlantic slave trade.\n \nBio:\nDavid L. Imbua is a Professor of Atlant
 ic History and Diaspora Studies at the University of Calabar\, Nigeria\, w
 here he serves as Head of the Department of History and International Stud
 ies and Deputy Director of the Institute for African and Asian Studies. A 
 distinguished historian of the Atlantic world\, slavery\, the Cross River 
 region\, and Nigerian historiography\, he has authored over 100 scholarly 
 publications. His award-winning book\, Intercourse and Crosscurrents in th
 e Atlantic World: Calabar-British Experience\, 17th–20th Centuries\, rec
 eived the 2015 Lapai Book Prize for Outstanding Historical Text in Interna
 tional History. He has contributed to UNESCO’s General History of Africa
  and collaborated with Professors Paul E. Lovejoy\, Nicholas Radburn\, and
  Randy J. Sparks on the landmark book\, The Notorious Massacre at Calabar 
 in 1767. A former valedictorian and overall best graduating student of the
  University of Calabar’s 2002 graduating class\, Professor Imbua current
 ly serves as President of the Historical Society of Nigeria\, the country
 ’s first professional association\, founded in 1955.\nModerator: Dr. Dam
 ilola Adebayo\, Department of History\, York University.\n\n\n\n\nDamilola
  Adebayo is an Associate Professor of History and a faculty member in the 
 Graduate Program in Science and Technology Studies (STS). He serves as Vic
 e President of the Canadian Association of African Studies and Associate E
 ditor of History and Technology. An award-winning historian of Anglophone 
 West Africa\, particularly Nigeria\, his research and teaching lie at the 
 intersection of the history of technology\, socioeconomic life\, and the r
 ole of international organizations in Africa. His work examines the socioe
 conomic life of Western technologies in African cities since the mid-ninet
 eenth century\, with particular attention to the varied contexts in which 
 energy\, communication\, and transportation technologies were introduced\,
  adopted\, appropriated\, hybridised\, reinvented\, or discarded by both e
 lite and everyday users\, and to how these technologies have been both a c
 ause and an effect of economic\, social\, and political change in African 
 societies. His work has earned international recognition\, including the M
 aurice Daumas Prize\, awarded by the International Committee for the Histo
 ry of Technology (ICOHTEC) for the best article in the history of technolo
 gy\, and the Bernard S. Finn IEEE History Prize\, awarded by the Society f
 or the History of Technology (SHOT) for the best article on the history of
  electrotechnology. His prize-winning scholarship has appeared in leading 
 historical journals\, including Past & Present. He earned a BA in History 
 from the University of Ibadan\, an MA in International History from the Ge
 neva Graduate Institute\, and a PhD in History from the University of Camb
 ridge\, where he was a Cambridge-Africa Scholar and recipient of the IEEE 
 Life Members’ Fellowship in the History of Electrical and Computing Tech
 nology. His book project\, Power and the People: Electricity and Urban Lif
 e in Nineteenth- and Twentieth-Century Nigeria\, contributes to broader de
 bates on infrastructure\, urbanization\, development\, state power\, moder
 nity\, and energy inequality.\n\n \n
LAST-MODIFIED:20261023
LOCATION:(3rd flr York Lanes\, Rm 314)
ORGANIZER;CN="The Harriet Tubman Institute":MAILTO:tubman@yorku.ca
PRIORITY:5
