SUMMARY: Kashikari-Inspired Tile Glazing Workshop | Misbah Ahmed & Tigerb
 aby Coffee & Clay
DESCRIPTION:Join sukoon space\, ceramic artist Misbah Ahmed https://misba
 hahmed.com/?utm_source=luma and Tigerbaby Coffee and Clay https://tigerba
 bycc.com/?utm_source=luma for an afternoon of tile glazing inspired by Ka
 shikari.\n\nTaking the colours and patterns of Kashikari tilework as a st
 arting point\, you’ll explore your own design on a handmade ceramic tile.
  Misbah will guide you through the glazing process\, working on an approx
 imately 7 × 7-inch tile that has been prepared and fired ahead of the wor
 kshop.\n\nSpend an afternoon making\, experimenting with pattern and conn
 ecting with others\, while creating a piece of your own. All glazing mate
 rials\, guided instruction and final kiln firing are included.\n\nAbout K
 ashikari\n\nKashikari is the art of glazed ceramic tilework\, with roots 
 in Persian ceramic traditions and a distinctive heritage in Multan\, Paki
 stan. Multani tilework is known for its cobalt blues\, turquoise and whit
 e\, combining intricate floral motifs and geometric patterns to decorate 
 shrines and historic buildings.\n\nIn this workshop\, we’ll take inspirat
 ion from that tradition as you glaze your own handmade\, pre-fired cerami
 c tile. Misbah will guide you through exploring colour and pattern\, with
  room to interpret the designs in your own way.\n\nArtist Bio\n\nMisbah A
 hmed is a Toronto-based artist and educator working across ceramics\, pai
 nting and printmaking. Born in Islamabad and raised in Canada\, she draws
  on Pakistani and South Asian stories\, family histories and archival res
 earch to explore belonging\, cultural change and our relationship with th
 e environment.\n\nHer work brings an interest in traditional crafts into 
 conversation with themes of memory\, resilience and the climate crisis. S
 he graduated from OCAD University’s Illustration program in 2018\, receiv
 ing its program medal\, and has taught there since 2020. She also teaches
  ceramics at the Gardiner Museum and founded Sehn Studio in 2024\, a Toro
 nto space for art-making\, classes and guest workshops. Her solo exhibiti
 ons include Dry Thunder at the Art Gallery of Burlington in 2025.
CATEGORIES:Workshop,Tigerbaby-Coffee-&-Clay
X-TKF-FEATURED-IMAGE:https://d3flpus5evl89n.cloudfront.net/6755f3e6a02aaf
 1c2599e757/6adbe3a2f1c2349eecae3c55/scaled_768.jpg
